1. d4 { Okay, keep playing 1.d5 } 1... d5 2. c4 { [%cal Gg8f6] } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 { It can be transposed with 3.e6 to a very classic way of playing against the queen's gambit, the declined Queen's Gambit. But, what I am telling you now to add more things to your repertoire is 3.g6 } { [%cal Gg7g6,Re7e6] } 3... g6 { The Grünfeld Defense is a popular and effective chess defense against 1.d4. A lot has been played, even at the world champions. The Grunfeld defense having so much versatility in the game has been highly appreciated by Grandmasters. Both Pachman, Botvinnik and Alekhine played it and today give names to many variants, this is why this Defense is so popular and is always an option to play. } 4. cxd5 { Capture the pawn } { [%cal Gf6d5] } 4... Nxd5 5. e4 { You must make a decision } { [%cal Gd5c3,Gd5b6] } 5... Nxc3 { This is the best, capture } 6. bxc3 { Now, play 6.Bg7 } { [%cal Gf8g7] } 6... Bg7 { This is all theory. } *

