1. d4 { Now, play 1.Nf6 } { [%cal Gg8f6] } 1... Nf6 { 1.Nf6 is also a VERY COMMON way to play against 1.e4, in fact it is the most common. } 2. c4 { Many times when playing 1.Nf3, your opponent will play c4 to gain more space.
To play the Benoni Defense, you must play c5 } { [%cal Gc7c5] } 2... c5 3. d5 { [%cal Ge7e6] } 3... e6 4. Nc3 { Capture the pawn } 4... exd5 5. cxd5 { How can you support the c5-pawn with another pawn in a firm way? } 5... d6 { The basic idea of the Benoni is to give up the center in exchange for unleashing a strong attack on the queen wing thanks to its majority of pawns in that sector.
Black's main ideas in this defense are: the queen plays a5 to press on the c3 square
black plays an early b5, often and as a main idea in the form of a gambit } *

